Source files do not carry their own license headers. This file is the
single statement that covers them.

Third-party components keep their own licenses. THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md
lists every one (Swift packages, JavaScript packages, voice models, tools
and captured data), with its licence and where its text is. It is
generated from docs/legal/third-party-components.json, and
scripts/third_party_notices.py fails when something is missing from it.
The notice texts themselves are in these files, which are the
authoritative copies:

- App/SpokenMarginsApp/Resources/Kitten/NOTICES.txt
  Kitten fallback voice: KittenTTS model, misaki lexicon and g2p_en
  weights (Apache-2.0), CMU Pronouncing Dictionary (CMUdict-LICENSE.txt
  in the same folder).
- App/SpokenMarginsApp/Resources/Supertonic/licenses/
  Supertonic code (MIT), Supertonic model (OpenRAIL-M), ONNX Runtime
  (MIT, plus its own third-party notices) and sherpa-onnx (Apache-2.0).
- App/SpokenMarginsApp/Resources/Legal/ZIPFoundation-LICENSE.txt
  ZIPFoundation (MIT).
- App/SharedResources/BundledJavaScript-NOTICES.txt and
  App/SharedResources/Defuddle-LICENSE.txt
  JavaScript packages compiled into the page-reading scripts
  (@xmldom/xmldom, defuddle, mathml-to-latex, temml, turndown). The
  share extension carries copies of the same files.
- Packages/SessionKit/Sources/SessionKitSupertonic/SupertonicRuntime.swift
  Adapted from Supertone Inc.'s Supertonic Swift example (MIT); its
  header says so.

Swift packages fetched at build time (for example
onnxruntime-swift-package-manager, listed in
Packages/SessionKit/Package.swift) are covered by their own licenses.
